WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:06.060
An actor isn't usually actually in harm's
way when performing horrific scenarios for

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:06.840
our entertainment.

00:00:07.510 --> 00:00:12.200
But some stars throw caution to the wind,
going above and beyond to ensure their performance

00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:13.740
conveys pure terror.

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:14.860
Is it worth it?

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:15.720
You decide.

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:20.539
How do you remake a Dario Argento classic
like Suspiria?

00:00:20.540 --> 00:00:24.760
If you're Dakota Johnson, the star of the
remake, the answer is that you commit.

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:26.260
To an unhealthy degree.

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:30.060
As Johnson told Elle, her experience of playing
the dancer:

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:32.420
"... f---ed me up so much that I had to go
to therapy."

00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:34.580
The shooting conditions were brutal.

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:38.340
The primary location was an abandoned hotel
atop a mountain.

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:43.400
The weather was dry and cold, resulting in
constant discomfort among the cast.

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:49.370
Plus, due to the 30 telephone poles on the
building's roof, there was supposedly electricity

00:00:49.370 --> 00:00:53.420
pulsing through the building to the point
that everyone was shocking each other.

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:55.440
What's Johnson done since then?

00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:59.590
She's starting her own production company,
hoping to create projects that showcase what

00:00:59.590 --> 00:01:00.660
she wants to do...

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:03.820
Which, presumably, include non-electrified
sets.

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:09.720
When film critic Roger Ebert asked Shelley
Duvall what it was like working with master

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:15.300
director Stanley Kubrick on The Shining, Duvall
responded bluntly, calling it "almost unbearable."

00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:21.120
Kubrick's film is an acclaimed horror classic,
but it arguably came at the cost of Duvall's

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:23.800
sanity, who called shooting it "excruciating."

00:01:24.470 --> 00:01:30.240
For nine months, she put in at least 12 hours
a day, screaming and crying, bearing the brunt

00:01:30.250 --> 00:01:36.570
of Kubrick's artistic impulses, one Hollywood
legend goes that Kubrick asked for over 120

00:01:36.570 --> 00:01:39.040
takes of a scene involving Duvall and a baseball
bat.

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:40.560
"Give me the bat."

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:41.680
"Stop it!"

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:43.280
"Give me the bat."

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:45.100
"Stay away from me!"

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:46.280
"Stop swinging the bat."

00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:47.080
“Please stop!”

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:48.240
“Give me the bat.”

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:52.420
And after her trials were complete, and the
film was released?

00:01:52.420 --> 00:01:56.780
Critics wrote of the film's genius in every
way, except for Duvall's performance.

00:01:57.080 --> 00:01:58.080
She told Ebert:

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:01.799
"The reviews were all about Kubrick, like
I wasn't there…

00:02:01.799 --> 00:02:06.670
Perhaps with a star director such as Kubrick,
I said, critics get mesmerized by his name

00:02:06.670 --> 00:02:07.680
and forget the actors."

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:14.620
Tobe Hooper's exploitation classic The Texas
Chainsaw Massacre is steeped in grimy authenticity.

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:19.580
It really looks like Marilyn Burns was stuck
in a slaughterhouse full of rotten corpses

00:02:19.580 --> 00:02:21.240
and tortured until she screamed.

00:02:21.740 --> 00:02:25.240
The reason it looks like this is… well,
she was.

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:28.540
The dinner scene when she was tied up and
taunted?

00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:32.300
Hooper tied her up and set the other actors
mercilessly on her.

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:36.980
In fact, an absurd number of the horrible
events that her character endures happened

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:38.230
to the actress herself.

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:41.980
The heroic moment when Burns escapes by jumping
through the window?

00:02:42.340 --> 00:02:47.120
Burns actually jumped through a window.Thankfully
she didn't get placed on a hook or have to

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:48.400
fight off actual cannibals.

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:55.420
The Exorcist is, to this day, such a picture
of absolute horror, your body might hurt from

00:02:55.420 --> 00:02:57.160
all the tension carried while watching.

00:02:57.860 --> 00:03:01.900
But it still wouldn't compare to what Ellen
Burstyn went through to play Chris, the poor

00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:03.720
mother of a possessed little girl.

00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:07.840
In one scene, Chris' daughter Regan pushes
her to the floor, hard.

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:13.160
After take one, Burstyn complained to director
William Friedkin that the stunt coordinator

00:03:13.160 --> 00:03:15.840
was pulling a rope tied to Burstyn too strongly.

00:03:16.280 --> 00:03:17.700
Burstyn told HuffPost Live:

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:20.000
"And Billy said, 'well it has to look real.'

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:23.519
And I said, 'I know it has to look real, but
I'm telling you, I could get hurt.'

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:25.840
And so he said, 'Okay, don't pull her so hard.'"

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:30.920
After that, Friedkin called action, and Burstyn
was slammed to the floor so hard she suffered

00:03:30.920 --> 00:03:32.280
permanent spinal injuries.

00:03:33.069 --> 00:03:36.380
Burstyn suspects Friedkin wanted a good-looking
take at all costs.

00:03:37.629 --> 00:03:42.809
During production on the 2003 horror film
Gothika, Robert Downey Jr. and Halle Berry

00:03:42.809 --> 00:03:44.400
were in the middle of an intense scene.

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:49.379
Downey was supposed to grab Berry's arm in
a certain, choreographed way, but instead,

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:53.280
he grabbed it about as wrong as you could
grab it, and it snapped.

00:03:55.220 --> 00:03:56.280
"Noooo!

00:03:56.680 --> 00:03:57.260
Nooooo!

00:03:57.560 --> 00:03:58.680
Noooo!"

00:03:58.860 --> 00:03:59.360
"Alright!"

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:01.740
"Owww!

00:04:01.880 --> 00:04:02.380
Nooooo!"

00:04:02.620 --> 00:04:07.099
Director Mathieu Kassovitz found it to be
a lucky break, as the pause in shooting gave

00:04:07.100 --> 00:04:10.700
him a chance to prep for upcoming action scenes
he didn't have time to plan.

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:13.120
Poltergeist is riddled with controversy.

00:04:13.700 --> 00:04:15.120
Was the production really cursed?

00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:18.360
Did Tobe Hooper or Steven Spielberg really
direct it?

00:04:19.020 --> 00:04:21.560
All of these questions may never have official
answers.

00:04:21.860 --> 00:04:26.680
But we do know one thing for certain: actor
JoBeth Williams, who played Diane Freeling,

00:04:26.680 --> 00:04:29.380
had a hauntingly miserable time making the
movie.

00:04:29.870 --> 00:04:35.040
In a Reddit Ask Me Anything session, Williams
called the shoot "physically very hard," especially

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:39.340
the moments when she was dropped into a pool
full of a gross, slimy concoction.

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:44.560
During one particularly yucky scene, Williams
shared the screen with some skeletons.

00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:45.600
She told Reddit:

00:04:45.700 --> 00:04:49.240
"When we were shooting, I thought the skeletons
were fake, I thought the prop department made

00:04:49.240 --> 00:04:49.740
them.

00:04:49.980 --> 00:04:53.960
But later I found out they were REAL skeletons
bought very cheaply.

00:04:54.140 --> 00:04:55.440
That really grossed me out."

00:04:56.020 --> 00:05:00.240
The supernatural seemed to have followed Williams
home away from the film as well.

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:04.160
When she returned to her rented apartment
after a day's shooting, all of the pictures

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:04.660
were crooked.

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:05.840
So she straightened them.

00:05:06.460 --> 00:05:09.260
However, the next day, the pictures were crooked
once again.

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:13.700
That's almost spookier than just about anything
that's actually in Poltergeist.

